    "lawCode": "Amd §§26-403 & 26-504, NYC Ad Cd; amd §5, Emerg Ten Prot Act of 1974; amd §2, Emerg Hous Rent Cont L; amd §1, Chap 21 of 1962",
    "lawSection": "New York City Administrative Code",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3317\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, the\nemergency tenant protection act of nineteen seventy-four, the\nemergency housing rent control law and the local emergency housing\nrent control act, in relation to establishing primary residency for\nvictims of domestic violence\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nTo prevent landlords from evicting tenants who are forced to flee\ntheir rent-controlled homes to escape domestic violence.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nThis bill amends § 26-403 and § 26-504 of the Administrative Code of\nthe City of New York; the Emergency Tenant Protection Act of 1974, the\nEmergency Housing Rent control Law, and the Local Emergency Housing\nRent Control Act to provide that victims of domestic violence as\ndefined in Social Services Law 459-a shall be deemed to occupy\nrent-regulated units as primary residences if they have been forced to\nleave the units because of such violence and assert an intent to\nreturn.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\nUnder current law, a residential unit must be maintained as a tenant's\nprimary residence in order for the unit to be covered by rent\nregulation statutes.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nVictims who flee their homes to escape abuse risk everything they\nhave: their economic security, their children and even their lives.\nAnd if they live in rent-regulated apartments, they also risk losing\ntheir homes. In New York city, landlords have successfully used the\nprimary residence requirements under rent control and rent\nstabilization laws to evict domestic violence victims who were forced\nto flee their homes to escape abuse. This bill will prevent landlords\nfrom evicting such victims.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n03/14/07 REFERRED TO HOUSING, CONSTRUCTION AND COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT\n01109/08 REFERRED TO HOUSING, CONSTRUCTION AND COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately, provided, however, that the\namendments to section 26-504 of the administrative code of the city of\nNew York made by section two of this act shall not affect the\nexpiration of such section and shall be deemed to expire therewith,\nand provided, further, the amendment to section 5 of section 4 of\nchapter 576 of the laws of 1974, constituting the emergency tenant\nprotection act of nine- teen seventy-four, shall expire and shall not\naffect the expiration of such act as provided in section 17 of chapter\n576 of the laws of 1974, as amended; and provided further that the\namendments to paragraph (1) of subdivision 2 of section 2 of the\nemergency housing rent control law made by section four of this act\nshall expire on the same date as such law and shall not effect the\nexpiration of such law as provided in subdivision 2 of section 1 of\nchapter 274 of the laws of 1946 as amended.\n",
