    "lawCode": "Amd §§26-403 & 26-504, NYC Ad Cd; amd §5, Emerg Ten Prot Act of 1974; amd §2, Emerg Hous Rent Cont L; amd §1, Chap 21 of 1962",
    "lawSection": "New York City Administrative Code",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S3317A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, the\nemergency tenant protection act of nineteen seventy-four, the\nemergency housing rent control law and the local emergency housing\nrent control act, in relation to establishing primary residency for\nvictims of domestic violence\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis bill would prevent landlords from evicting tenants who leave\ntheir rent-controlled homes to escape domestic violence.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nThis bill amends § 26-403 and § 26-504 of the Administrative Code of\nthe City of New York; the Emergency Tenant Protection Act of 1974, the\nEmergency Housing Rent Control Law, and the Local Emergency Housing\nRent Control Act by adding language to each amended section that\npermits victims of domestic violence to maintain primary residence of\na rent-regulated unit under the following circumstances.  If a tenant\nis a victim of domestic violence as defined in Social Services Law\n459-a, has left the housing unit because of such violence, and asserts\nan intent to return, the tenant shall be deemed to occupy the\nrent-regulated units as his or her primary residence.\n\nThe A-print changes the phrase \"has been forced to leave\" to the\nphrase \"has left\" throughout the bill. There are no other changes in\nthe A-print.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nVictims who flee their homes to escape abuse risk everything they\nhave: their economic security, their children and even their lives.\nAnd if they live in rent-regulated apartments, they also risk losing\ntheir homes. In New York City, landlords have successfully used the\nprimary residence requirements under rent control and rent\nstabilization laws to evict domestic violence victims who have fled\ntheir homes to escape abuse. This bill will prevent landlords from\nevicting such victims.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nA.7099 (2007-08); no Assembly introduction in 2005-2006, but\nintroduced in Senate as S.3708; A.7344-A/S.3420-A (2003-2004; passed\nAssembly 2004).\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately, provided, however, that the\nexpiration dates of the various laws it amends shall not be affected\nby the amendments.\n",
